# ENGINE-UNIVERSAL-SERVICES.17 slice 4 — G5: G4's one-difference control.
#
# Byte-for-byte `g4_trailing_guard_needs_a_clone.ebnf` except that the TRAILING guard is removed
# (the per-iteration guard stays — this is G2's `prim` under G4's two holders):
#
#   G4:  prim := prim_base ( prim_suffix &( tick lparen lit rparen ) )* &( tick lparen lit rparen )
#   G5:  prim := prim_base ( prim_suffix &( tick lparen lit rparen ) )*
#
# REQUIRED VERDICT: **ACCEPT** on `e7` (`k = n;`), the input G4 rejects.
#
# ⛔ WHY IT IS MANDATORY. Without it, G4's REJECT has two readings — *"the trailing guard broke the
# residual-free holder"* and *"this shape cannot parse `k = n;` at all"* — and only the first is a
# finding. G5 exhibits the parse G4's guard refused, so the REJECT can only be the guard.
#
# ⭐ And it doubles as the scoping cost statement: G5 is what the OTHER call site must receive. Two
# holders of the same rule need two different clones — G4's body reached from `outer_cast`, G5's
# body reached from the bare holder. That is the price of the trailing guard, and it is the same
# clone the loop guard already pays for, not a second one.
@entry: true
scratch := kw_k eq outer_cast semi
         | kw_k eq prim semi
outer_cast := ct tick lparen lit rparen
ct := kw | prim
prim := prim_base ( prim_suffix &( tick lparen lit rparen ) )*
prim_base := lit | cast_seed
cast_seed := kw tick lparen lit rparen
prim_suffix := tick lparen lit rparen

lit := trivia /n/
kw := trivia /t/
kw_k := trivia /k/
eq := trivia "="
semi := trivia ";"
tick := trivia "'"
lparen := trivia "("
rparen := trivia ")"

@branch_policy: priority_first
trivia := (line_comment | block_comment)*
line_comment := /\/\/[^\n]*(\n|$)/
block_comment := /\/\*([^*]|\*+[^*\/])*\*+\//
